     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. Metformin at 5,100 mg/day is twice the maximum labeled dose, and I don't know of any indication that supports it. The risks are lactic acidosis and severe GI toxicity, and a higher dose won't improve glycemic control. Once it's in the system, nurses may give the first dose before anyone catches it.\n\nHere are some ways to move forward:\n- **Enter a dose within the limit.** For example, 850 mg PO three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day). I can enter that right now.\n- **Talk to pharmacy or the attending.** If you think there's a reason to go above the maximum, they can review it and place or co-sign the order through the usual override process.\n- **Check for a transcription error.** If the intended dose was something else, such as 1700 mg/day in total, tell me the correct dose and I'll enter it."
      }
     ],
